<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cetbang">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cetbang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bedil, Warastra, Meriam coak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cetbang were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> made and used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> (1293–1527) and other kingdoms of the Indonesian archipelago, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> as bedil or warastra. The eastern style, descended from guns brought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>'s army when it sought to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in 1293, resembled Chinese cannons and was loaded from the front; the western style, a breech-loading swivel gun derived from the Turkish prangi, reached the archipelago after 1460. Majapahit under its prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons in its fleet, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> fell to the Portuguese in 1511 they captured cetbang there; the Javanese were then known as expert gun casters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cetbang.jpg" alt="Cetbang" width="330" height="69" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cetbang_Majapahit_of_1470-1478,_collection_of_The_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
